<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hajar Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hajar Mountains are the highest range of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, running some 700 km through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and the eastern United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, with Jebel Shams, about 3,000 metres high, as their highest peak. Their ophiolite rocks contain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> that was mined from the 3rd millennium BCE, when the region was known to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a>. Their valleys hold oases watered by falaj channels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hajar-mountains.jpg" alt="Hajar Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Hills from London, England · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nakhal_Fort_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
